        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Nuragic civilisation</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, with about one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuraghe">nuraghe</a> every three square kilometres; key sites <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-nuraxi">Su Nuraxi</a> at Barumini, Santu Antine at Torralba, Nuraghe Losa, Nuraghe Arrubiu at Orroli, the sanctuary of Santa Vittoria at Serri, the holy well of Santa Cristina at Paulilatino, Mont’e Prama near Cabras</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Bonnanaro culture and the first proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuraghe">nuraghi</a> (c. 1800–1600 BCE); the classic nuraghi from the middle of the 2nd millennium BCE; no new nuraghi after the 12th–11th century BCE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> “era of the aristocracies” (900–500 BCE); Carthaginian conquest of the coasts in the 6th century BCE; Roman rule from 238 BCE, with survival in the interior at least into the early Imperial period</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuraghe">nuraghi</a>, stone towers with vaulted tholos chambers; holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> for a water cult; giants’ graves; the bronze statuettes called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuragic-bronze-statuettes">bronzetti</a>; the Giants of Mont’e Prama; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and bronze metalwork traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuraghe">nuraghi</a>, villages, sanctuaries and tombs (Giovanni Lilliu’s synthesis of 1966 and later work); Nuragic pottery and bronzes found abroad; Greek and Roman writers such as Pseudo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Justin; ancient DNA (2020 and later); no written records but a few possible short inscriptions</span></div>
        </div>
