    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Nuragic civilisation</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuraghe.jpg" alt="Nuraghe" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Nuraghe <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jack Aubrey, CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 3,800 years ago, people on Sardinia began to build stone towers called nuraghi.</p><p>Chiefs lived in the biggest towers, and others lived nearby in round stone houses.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Water was at the heart of their religion, and they built holy wells over sacred springs.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuragic-bronze-statuettes.jpg" alt="Nuragic bronze statuettes" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Nuragic bronze statuettes <span class="kid-tale-cr">Shardan, CC BY 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their metalworkers cast small bronze statues of chiefs, warriors, musicians, animals and ships.</p><p>Sardinia was rich in copper, and its people became some of Europe’s main metal makers.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Bronze needs tin, which Sardinia hardly had, so they traded for it.</p><p>Their pottery and bronzes travelled by sea to the Aegean, Cyprus, Spain and Italy.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mercenary-war.jpg" alt="Mercenary War" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mercenary War <span class="kid-tale-cr">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Later, Carthage took the coasts, and then Rome took the whole island.</p><p>Nuragic ways lasted long in the mountains, and more than 7,000 towers still dot Sardinia.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Nuragic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuraghe">nuraghe</a>, a stone tower with vaulted inner chambers, in large numbers from about 1800 BCE, and more than 7,000 still dot the island.</li><li>They were skilled metalworkers, among the main metal producers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and their pottery and bronzes reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the Etruscan cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</li><li>Ancient DNA shows strong continuity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farmers and almost no steppe-related ancestry, unlike many other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Giants of Mont’e Prama, stone statues up to 2.5 metres tall found near Cabras in 1974, have been dated between the 11th and 8th centuries BCE; if that is confirmed, they would be the oldest statues of people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> after those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

